#!/usr/bin/env node
/**
 * An anchored edit, applied all-or-nothing and syntax-checked, instead of a scratchpad
 * patch script written fresh for every change.
 *
 *     b7e-apply --file public/app.js --anchor "old text" --to "new text"
 *     b7e-apply --patch edits.yaml
 *     b7e-apply --patch edits.yaml --dry
 *
 * bc-khoe.26 wrote fourteen of these scripts by hand, bc-khoe.27.3 wrote seven more,
 * bc-khoe.27.2 wrote nine and started only after `Edit` twice refused a comment-heavy
 * block of public/app.js with "String to replace not found in file". Every one opened
 * with the same five lines: read the file, assert an anchor string appears exactly
 * once, replace it, write, then `node --check` or run the file's own suite. Two of the
 * hand-rolled ones failed on their own escaping and cost a further two round trips —
 * the per-change decision (which anchor, what to put in its place) is the work, and
 * retyping the applier around it, badly, each time, is not.
 *
 * ## Two ways in
 *
 * One pair, on the command line: `--file <path> --anchor <old> --to <new>`. Several,
 * across one file or several, in a patch: `--patch <file.yaml|file.json>`, or piped on
 * stdin when `--patch` names no file. A patch is a flat list — `[{file, anchor, to}, …]`
 * — or `{ edits: [...] }`; YAML parses JSON too; block scalars (`to: |`) are how a
 * multi-line replacement stays readable, which is the whole reason a patch file beats
 * a shell argument for anything bigger than a one-liner.
 *
 * ## All-or-nothing, and why the check comes before the write
 *
 * Every anchor in the patch is resolved against an in-memory copy of its file before a
 * single byte reaches disk, in order — edits to the same file chain, so anchor 2 sees
 * anchor 1's replacement already applied. The first anchor that matches zero times or
 * more than once stops the whole patch there: the count, the nearest line by word
 * overlap (a free hint that the anchor drifted since it was read, not a promise of the
 * right line), and nothing written anywhere, including files whose own edits had
 * already resolved cleanly. A `.js`/`.mjs`/`.cjs` file is then checked with `node
 * --check` against its *computed* text — still before any write — because a syntax
 * error caught here names the file; caught by the next `npm test` it is 38 suites in,
 * reading as an unrelated regression (lib/conflicted.js, which this reuses, is the
 * account of exactly that happening to a committed merge).
 *
 * ## An edit can ask for more than one occurrence
 *
 * `occurrences(...) !== 1` was always an error, so "replace all 3 of these" needed three
 * separate anchors or a hand script. An edit may instead carry `count: N` (bc-dgx7.179);
 * the edit then asserts the anchor matches exactly N times and replaces all N — any other
 * number still fails the whole patch, naming the edit and both the expected and the found
 * count. `count` defaults to 1, so every existing patch means exactly what it always did.
 *
 * ## What --dry reports, and what only a real run prints
 *
 * `--dry` no longer stops at the first bad anchor: it keeps going through the whole patch
 * (skipping only the failed edit's own replacement, so files downstream of it are checked
 * against text as it stood before that edit) and prints one status line per edit — ok or
 * FAIL, with expected-vs-found on a failure — so a dry run over a large patch shows every
 * problem in one pass rather than one round trip per anchor. A real run keeps stopping at
 * the first failure, since there is no reason to keep resolving a patch it is about to
 * write. A successful real run additionally prints a plain-whitespace word-count delta for
 * each file it changed (`path  +12 words` / `path  -340 words`) — the prose sessions this
 * was filed for were cutting chapters to a word target and re-running `wc -w` by hand
 * after every pass.
 *
 * ## Where this lives, and why the filename has no `.js`
 *
 * The bead that filed this says `bin/b7e-apply.js` and an allowlist line
 * `Bash(b7e-apply:*)`. Those two cannot both be true: `lib/foundation.js` puts the main
 * checkout's `bin/` on PATH directly (`BIN`, no `npm link`), which resolves a command
 * by the exact name typed — `bin/beadcause-memory` answers to `beadcause-memory`, but
 * `bin/deliver.js` does not answer to `beadcause-deliver` (bc-jlop; measured — `which
 * beadcause-deliver` finds nothing). An allowlist entry matches the command as typed,
 * so a file named `b7e-apply.js` could only ever be reached as `b7e-apply.js`, never as
 * `b7e-apply`. This file has no extension so the name and the allowlist agree; it is
 * still ESM because `package.json` says `"type": "module"` and node infers that for an
 * extensionless entry point. bc-dgx7.2 is the bead that is meant to decide this
 * convention properly and it is blocked behind bc-jlop; neither had landed when this
 * shipped, so this decided it the way the standing note on the question says to.
 *
 * The grant argument, as it was made when this tool was added — moved here verbatim from
 * lib/tooldecl.js by bc-fmk4v, so the reason sits beside the declaration it argues for and
 * adding a tool appends to no shared file. "This list" is DEFAULT_TOOL_LIST; "above",
 * "below" and "beside" name the order lib/tooldecl.js kept these in, which
 * `git show 7c47d7d77:lib/tooldecl.js` still reads whole.
 *
 * b7e-apply (bc-khoe.27.6) is deliberately NOT on this list. This is "the read-only
 * surface every reply agent gets" and its one consumer is `dispatch` (lib/foundation.js)
 * — a single turn that answers a phone comment with a `bd comment` and exits, with no
 * git tools, no branch of its own and no oversight loop. b7e-apply writes to whatever
 * checkout it is run in; granting it here would let a one-shot comment-answerer edit
 * the tracked tree of a repo it does not own mid-session, with no path to commit or
 * review the result. test/grants.mjs is what catches an addition like this — it fails
 * an unclassified grant on sight — and the classification that belongs in lib/grants.js
 * for a live grant is not the right answer to that failure here; not granting it is.
 *
 * @grant excluded
 */
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';
import YAML from 'yaml';
import { parseError } from '../lib/conflicted.js';

const PARSEABLE = new Set(['.js', '.mjs', '.cjs']);

function arg(...names) {
  for (const n of names) {
    const i = process.argv.indexOf(n);
    if (i > -1) return process.argv[i + 1];
  }
  return undefined;
}
const has = (n) => process.argv.includes(n);
const warn = (msg) => console.error(`b7e-apply: ${msg}`);

const USAGE = [
  'usage: b7e-apply --file <path> --anchor <old> --to <new> [--count N] [--dry]',
  '       b7e-apply --patch <edits.yaml|edits.json> [--dry]',
  '       b7e-apply --patch - [--dry]        # patch piped on stdin',
  '',
  'a patch is a YAML or JSON list of { file, anchor, to, count? }, or { edits: [...] }',
  'count defaults to 1 — the anchor must match exactly that many times, and all are replaced',
];

// bc-dgx7.31: checked before readEdits() runs — that function falls through to
// `fs.readFileSync(0, ...)` whenever no --file/--patch is given and stdin is not a
// TTY, so `b7e-apply --help` with a closed or piped stdin used to read (or wait on)
// stdin and then report "no patch given" as an error, exit 1, rather than answering.
if (has('--help') || has('-h')) {
  console.log(USAGE.join('\n'));
  process.exit(0);
}

/** A `count` field, in either the single-pair or patch shape: undefined means 1. */
function parseCount(raw) {
  if (raw === undefined || raw === null) return { count: 1 };
  const n = typeof raw === 'number' ? raw : Number(raw);
  if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n < 1) return { error: `count must be a positive whole number — got ${JSON.stringify(raw)}` };
  return { count: n };
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------- reading the patch */

/**
 * The list of edits, from whichever of the two shapes was used — never both, because a
 * single pair and a patch answer the same question twice and there is no honest way to
 * merge disagreement between them.
 */
function readEdits() {
  const single = { file: arg('--file'), anchor: arg('--anchor'), to: arg('--to') };
  const singleGiven = single.file != null || single.anchor != null || single.to != null;
  const patchArg = arg('--patch');

  if (singleGiven && patchArg !== undefined) {
    return { error: '--file/--anchor/--to and --patch are two different ways in — use one' };
  }

  if (singleGiven) {
    if (single.file == null || single.anchor == null || single.to == null) {
      return { error: 'a single edit needs --file, --anchor and --to all three' };
    }
    const { count, error } = parseCount(arg('--count'));
    if (error) return { error };
    return { edits: [{ ...single, count }] };
  }

  if (patchArg === undefined && process.stdin.isTTY) {
    return { error: 'no patch given — pass --file/--anchor/--to, --patch <file>, or pipe one on stdin' };
  }

  const raw = patchArg && patchArg !== '-' ? fs.readFileSync(patchArg, 'utf8') : fs.readFileSync(0, 'utf8');
  if (!raw.trim()) return { error: 'no patch given — pass --file/--anchor/--to, --patch <file>, or pipe one on stdin' };
  let spec;
  try {
    spec = YAML.parse(raw);
  } catch (err) {
    return { error: `patch is not valid YAML — ${err.message.split('\n')[0]}` };
  }
  const edits = Array.isArray(spec) ? spec : spec?.edits;
  if (!Array.isArray(edits) || !edits.length) return { error: 'patch has no edits — expected a list, or { edits: [...] }' };
  const withCounts = [];
  for (const [i, e] of edits.entries()) {
    if (!e || typeof e.file !== 'string' || typeof e.anchor !== 'string' || typeof e.to !== 'string') {
      return { error: `edit ${i + 1} needs file, anchor and to, all strings — got ${JSON.stringify(e)}` };
    }
    const { count, error } = parseCount(e.count);
    if (error) return { error: `edit ${i + 1} (${e.file}): ${error}` };
    withCounts.push({ ...e, count });
  }
  return { edits: withCounts };
}

/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- anchor matching */

/** How many times `anchor` occurs in `text`. */
function occurrences(text, anchor) {
  if (!anchor) return 0;
  return text.split(anchor).length - 1;
}

/** `text` with every occurrence of `anchor` replaced by `to`. */
function replaceAll(text, anchor, to) {
  return text.split(anchor).join(to);
}

/** The 1-indexed line the first match of `anchor` starts on. */
function lineOf(text, anchor) {
  const idx = text.indexOf(anchor);
  if (idx < 0) return null;
  return text.slice(0, idx).split('\n').length;
}

/** Plain whitespace word count — the same thing `wc -w` counts. */
function wordCount(text) {
  const words = text.match(/\S+/g);
  return words ? words.length : 0;
}

/**
 * When an anchor matches nothing, the line in `text` whose words overlap its first
 * non-blank line the most — a cheap hint ("drifted since you read it" vs. "wrong file
 * entirely"), not a claim about which line is actually meant. Word-overlap rather than
 * an edit distance because this has to run over a 24,700-line README in well under a
 * second, and a Jaccard score per line is one pass with no nested scan.
 */
function nearestMiss(text, anchor) {
  const probe = anchor.split('\n').find((l) => l.trim()) || anchor;
  const probeWords = new Set(probe.match(/\w+/g) || []);
  if (!probeWords.size) return null;
  const lines = text.split('\n');
  let best = null;
  for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
    const words = lines[i].match(/\w+/g);
    if (!words || !words.length) continue;
    const lineWords = new Set(words);
    let hit = 0;
    for (const w of probeWords) if (lineWords.has(w)) hit += 1;
    if (!hit) continue;
    const union = new Set([...probeWords, ...lineWords]).size;
    const score = hit / union;
    if (!best || score > best.score) best = { score, line: i + 1, text: lines[i].trim() };
  }
  return best;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------- main */

function fail(code, msg) {
  warn(msg);
  process.exit(code);
}

/** The mismatch message shared by a real-run abort and a --dry status line. */
function mismatchMessage(e, count, text) {
  const snippet = e.anchor.length > 80 ? `${e.anchor.slice(0, 80)}…` : e.anchor;
  const near = count === 0 ? nearestMiss(text, e.anchor) : null;
  const nearMsg = near ? ` — nearest: ${e.file}:${near.line} "${near.text.length > 80 ? `${near.text.slice(0, 80)}…` : near.text}"` : '';
  return `${e.file}: expected ${e.count}, found ${count} (anchor matched ${count} times): "${snippet}"${nearMsg}`;
}

const parsed = readEdits();
if (parsed.error) {
  warn(parsed.error);
  console.error(USAGE.join('\n'));
  process.exit(1);
}
const { edits } = parsed;
const dryRun = has('--dry');

// One in-memory copy per file, read once and threaded through every edit against it —
// so edit 2 of a file sees edit 1's replacement, and nothing is written until every
// edit in the whole patch has resolved. originalByFile keeps the as-read text so a
// successful write can report each file's word-count delta against it.
const byFile = new Map();
const originalByFile = new Map();
const report = [];
let anyEditFailed = false;

for (const e of edits) {
  const abs = path.resolve(e.file);
  if (!byFile.has(abs)) {
    if (!fs.existsSync(abs)) fail(2, `${e.file}: no such file`);
    const text = fs.readFileSync(abs, 'utf8');
    byFile.set(abs, text);
    originalByFile.set(abs, text);
  }
  const text = byFile.get(abs);
  const count = occurrences(text, e.anchor);
  if (count !== e.count) {
    const msg = mismatchMessage(e, count, text);
    if (!dryRun) fail(3, `${msg} — nothing written`);
    anyEditFailed = true;
    report.push({ file: e.file, status: 'FAIL', detail: msg });
    continue;
  }
  report.push({ file: e.file, status: 'ok', line: lineOf(text, e.anchor), charsIn: e.anchor.length, charsOut: e.to.length });
  byFile.set(abs, replaceAll(text, e.anchor, e.to));
}

if (dryRun && anyEditFailed) {
  for (const r of report) {
    if (r.status === 'FAIL') console.log(`${r.file}  FAIL — ${r.detail}`);
    else console.log(`${r.file}:${r.line}  ${r.charsIn} in, ${r.charsOut} out  ok`);
  }
  const failCount = report.filter((r) => r.status === 'FAIL').length;
  warn(`${failCount} of ${edits.length} edit(s) would fail — nothing written`);
  process.exit(3);
}

// The target's own gate, run against the computed text — still before any write.
for (const [abs, text] of byFile) {
  if (!PARSEABLE.has(path.extname(abs))) continue;
  const err = parseError(text, abs);
  if (err) fail(4, `${path.relative(process.cwd(), abs)}: does not parse — ${err} — nothing written`);
}

for (const r of report) {
  console.log(dryRun ? `${r.file}:${r.line}  ${r.charsIn} in, ${r.charsOut} out  ok` : `${r.file}:${r.line}  ${r.charsIn} in, ${r.charsOut} out`);
}

if (dryRun) process.exit(0);

for (const [abs, text] of byFile) fs.writeFileSync(abs, text);

for (const [abs, text] of byFile) {
  const before = wordCount(originalByFile.get(abs));
  const after = wordCount(text);
  const delta = after - before;
  if (delta === 0) continue;
  const sign = delta > 0 ? '+' : '-';
  console.log(`${path.relative(process.cwd(), abs)}  ${sign}${Math.abs(delta)} words`);
}
